This aircraft is a JSBSim model of NASA's Ingenuity Mars Helicopter, written by PteroLabs in 2026, with NASA's own
3D model as its meshes.

The flight model -- ingenuity.xml, the files it assembles, Engines/ and Systems/ -- is PteroLabs' work, distributed
under the GNU General Public License v3.0; see LICENSE in this folder for its text. Its numbers come from:

  NASA/TM-20240001510, Koning and Dominguez, "Ingenuity Mars Helicopter rotor geometry" (February 2024): rotor
  diameter 1.21 m, two blades per rotor, design 2,600 rpm and 2,800 rpm maximum, thrust-weighted solidity 0.14782
  (the chord), disk area 1.150 m2, rotor spacing 0.099 m, mass 1.8 kg, the hingeless hub, and the twist and chord
  distributions the single twist value collapses.
    https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20240001510

  Koning, Johnson and Grip, "Improved Mars Helicopter Aerodynamic Rotor Model for Comprehensive Analyses", AIAA
  Journal Vol. 57 No. 9 (2019); open preprint from the 44th European Rotorcraft Forum (2018): the "Mars Condition 2"
  atmosphere (0.017 kg/m3, 223.2 K) the model was tuned against.
    https://rotorcraft.arc.nasa.gov/Publications/files/Koning_Johnson_Grip_Improved_Mars_Helicopter.pdf

  Grip et al., "Flight Control System for NASA's Mars Helicopter", AIAA 2019-1289: the control layout (a coaxial
  pair, yaw by differential collective).

  JPL, "Mars Helicopter Ingenuity" fact sheet (JPL 400-1715 Rev. 1, April 2020): mass 4 lb (1.8 kg), height about
  0.49 m, "about 2,400 rpm". NASA blog "Flying on Mars Is Getting Harder and Harder" (September 2021): 2,537 rpm
  flown, 2,700 rpm on Flight 14, 2,800 rpm spin test, surface density 0.0145-0.0185 kg/m3 falling to 0.012.

Chosen, with no source, and marked so where they appear: the blade lift-curve slope, the hinge-equivalent offset,
the blade flapping, mass and polar moments, the inflow lag, the vehicle inertias, the CG height above the feet, the
leg spring, damping and friction, the body drag coefficient, the motor power, the collective range and the cyclic
limits. README.md lists what is known to be wrong with the model as it stands.

The meshes are NASA's Ingenuity 3D model, credit NASA/JPL-Caltech, under the NASA media usage guidelines; NASA
does not endorse PteroSim. meshes/NOTICE says what was cut and what was removed (the NASA insignia and JPL logotype).
    https://science.nasa.gov/resource/mars-ingenuity-helicopter-3d-model/
    https://www.nasa.gov/nasa-brand-center/images-and-media/

The ArduPilot parameter file (firmwares/ardupilot_ingenuity.param) carries ArduPilot's own SITL defaults for a
dual-rotor helicopter (Tools/autotest/default_params/copter-heli.parm and copter-heli-dual.parm, ArduPilot commit
b7ba8bca02) with PteroLabs' vehicle values on top. ArduPilot is distributed under the GNU General Public License v3.0.
